['Question: Describe the patient experience.  ', 'Answer: The patient noticed several painless, erythematous nodules on her right lower extremity. After the skin lesions appeared, low-grade fever (up to 37.6°C) was noticed. A diagnosis of pyoderma gangrenosum (with possible bacterial contamination) was considered and treatment with infliximab initiated. Ten days after the second infusion of infliximab, the patient noticed worsening of the skin lesions along with high fever (38.7°C). An extensive search for infectious agents did not initially reveal a clear source. A skin biopsy showed findings consistent with a granulomatous inflammatory process of the skin, without necrosis. Treatment was initiated with intravenous cefoxitin, clarithromycin, and amikacin. Two weeks later the patient was significantly improved. Following the identification of the microorganism as Mycobacterium chelonae and susceptibility testing, she was discharged home on clarithromycin. Upon several follow-up visits, the patient remained afebrile and the cutaneous lesions were gradually eliminated.']
['Question: Did you notice any symptoms, such as a fever, cough, or respiratory issues?  ', 'Answer: A month and a half later, low-grade fever (up to 37.6°C) was noticed. Ten days after the second infusion of infliximab, the patient noticed worsening of the skin lesions along with high fever (38.7°C). As the patient experienced persistent fever (37.8 to 38.2°C), we performed extensive search for infectious agents.']
