['Question: Describe the patient experience.', 'Answer: Before referral to our department of paediatric surgery, a then 7-day-old girl presented with moaning and fever to another hospital. The perinatal period was uneventful. On admission, physical examination revealed no abnormalities except tachypnoea. Laboratory tests showed elevated infection parameters. Treatment with broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics (gentamicin and amoxicillin/clavulanic acid) was started immediately. Several days later the blood culture proved positive for Escherichia coli and antibiotic treatment was switched to amoxicillin only. Cerebral fluid cultures were negative. The infection parameters and the clinical symptoms improved. After 17 days, the girl was discharged with oral antibiotics that were continued for another 2 weeks. At 17 days after discharge, during a routine follow-up, the infection parameters had again risen. There were no symptoms of infection and physical examination did not reveal an apparent focus of infection. Abdominal US was performed followed by antigranulocyte antibody-scintigraphy. The child was subsequently referred to our hospital for further treatment. Intravenous antibiotics were started. Repeat abdominal US confirmed a hypoechoic mass. The mass was drained percutaneously under US guidance with an 8-French catheter and pus was retrieved. Additionally, a central venous catheter was placed for antibiotic treatment. The drain remained in situ until pus production had ceased 3 days later. Laboratory tests showed normalising infection parameters. At 6 days after drainage, abdominal US showed a residual mass. After another day, the central venous catheter had to be removed due to infection and oral administration of amoxicillin was started. The girl was discharged 2 days later. Antibiotics were ceased 10 days after drainage and further recovery was uneventful. At 5-months follow-up, no complications had occurred and abdominal US showed a normal left adrenal gland.']
