["Question: What's the direct evidence that points to this diagnosis?  ", 'Answer: A cerebriform connective tissue overgrowth was noted on the plantar and lateral aspects of the right foot. On T1-weighted imaging (T1WI), a cerebriform connective tissue overgrowth or nevus was identified on both the plantar and lateral aspects of the right foot, indicating an irregular growth pattern in this region.']
['Question: What’s the imaging (only provided the figure explanation here) suggest?  ', 'Answer: On T1-weighted imaging (T1WI), a cerebriform connective tissue overgrowth or nevus was identified on both the plantar and lateral aspects of the right foot, indicating an irregular growth pattern in this region. Additionally, the right foot appeared enlarged in size, suggesting macrodactyly or foot overgrowth. Furthermore, a visible discrepancy in length and size was noted between the affected leg (right) and the contralateral limb (left), indicating asymmetrical growth. In the thigh, muscle atrophy was evident, accompanied by hypertrophy of the intermuscular and subcutaneous fat planes, which may have contributed to the observed differences in leg size. Similarly, hypertrophy of subcutaneous fat and fatty infiltration within the calf muscles were observed. Moreover, both fibulas displayed inward bowing, indicating structural abnormalities in the lower limb bones. On right lower limb contrast-enhanced angiography and venography, several significant findings were observed. Enlargement was noted in the right superficial femoral, popliteal, and posterior tibial arteries, indicating increased arterial size in these regions. Additionally, feeders originating from the posterior tibial artery were identified in the lower third of the leg, alongside prominent vascular channels on the posterolateral aspect of the right leg and foot, as well as abnormal vessels within the foot itself. Furthermore, dilation was observed in the lower third of the superficial femoral and popliteal veins. A prominent long saphenous vein was also evident. Multiple varicosities were detected along the anterior and lateral aspects of the thigh, as well as the anterior, lateral, and posterior aspects of the leg within the subcutaneous plane.']
['Question: What’s the examination suggest?  ', 'Answer: Upon clinical examination, inversion of the right foot was observed, along with a limb length discrepancy where the right forearm and hand were longer than the left, and the right foot was longer than the left. A cerebriform connective tissue overgrowth was noted on the plantar and lateral aspects of the right foot. A radiograph of the lumbar spine revealed levoconvex scoliosis.']
